# stdlib.toml tutorial: parse service config and validate required settings

This short guide shows how to use `stdlib/toml.ltl` (`toml.parse`) to read a service configuration and check required settings.

## Pinned fixture

`examples/fixtures/stdlib_toml_service.toml`:
```toml
[service]
name = "billing-api"
port = 8080
enabled = true
banner = "ready #1"

[database]
url = "postgres://localhost/app"
```

Notes:
- `banner = "ready #1"` contains a quoted `#` that must be preserved as part of the string value (not treated as a comment).
- `service.api_key` is intentionally missing to demonstrate required-key reporting.

## Runnable example

`examples/stdlib_toml_service_config.ltl` parses the fixture and validates:
- `service.port == 8080`
- `service.enabled == true`
- quoted `#` preserved in `service.banner`
- reports missing required key `service.api_key`

### Run

```bash
lateralus run examples/stdlib_toml_service_config.ltl
```

### Expected output (pinned)

```text
[PASS] service.port == 8080
[PASS] service.enabled == true
[PASS] quoted # preserved in banner
[FAIL_REPORT] missing required key: service.api_key
PINNED_TOML_OK
```

`PINNED_TOML_OK` indicates the required checks passed and the missing key was correctly reported.
